A||||3||GT GT 87||Reusing a flywheel||||1||
Z||000000||GT GT 87||05-22-2001||05:32 PM||||I will have a 3.2 together soon. I need to think about a clutch and flywheel. I have a '86 F.W. that I can have neutrally balanced.  But my question -------- If I have it resurfaced , does it have to be shimmed from the crank to keep all dimensions the same from the through bearing ???????????  If i used a pressure plate and disc from V-8 Archie , would that fit the dimensions inside the V-6 bellhousing ????       thanks....||||reg||1
Z||000001||Taijiguy||05-23-2001||07:55 AM||||None of that should matter. You should be able to bolt it right up, since the clutch and pressure plate are attached right to the clutch face. And the starter and flywheel line up based on the flywheel being attached to the crankshaft. Shimming the flywheel could possibly cause problems, since you would be moving it out to the end of the starter drive gear. The hydraulic clutch release system should easily adjust itself for any change in the clutch/pressure plate movement. Shouldn't be any problem from a physical standpoint. Can't answer about Archie's stuff, since I've never used any of it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT GT 87||05-23-2001||10:16 PM||||Thanks T-Guy ..I love the picture you sent me of your Holley carb. on your V-6 !!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by GT GT 87 (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||cadaver||05-23-2001||11:03 PM||||No after you surface a flywheel you dont shim anything.<BR>Yes the clutch plate and cover sit a little closer to the engine. That is taken care of when you adjust the clutch. Of in the case of a hydralic it sets itself.<BR>The starter to flywheel dimension does not change.<BR>I dont know if the v6 had starter shims like the v8's do. Those you do need if they were there.||||reg||1
